# Files the build WRITES OVER, and that ipnxclean puts back.
#
# ipnxbuild's derived list is a set difference of NAMES, so it sees a file that
# appeared and is blind to one that CHANGED.  Every row below was on the tapes
# and is rewritten in place by a rule that is the tape's own, so deleting them
# would be wrong, and leaving them rewritten is what makes a tree that has
# built and cleaned stop matching the archive it came from.  ipnxbuild copies
# each aside before it builds anything; ipnxclean copies it back.
#
# NOT ONE ROW WAS GUESSED.  The first seven came from diffing a machine that
# had run ipnxbuild and ipnxclean against an archive extracted straight from
# the tapes.  The other eighteen the BUILD ITSELF named: ipnxbuild drops a
# stamp before building and afterwards asks find(1) for every file under the
# ten roots newer than it, subtracts the derived set, and prints whatever is
# left that is not listed here.  It printed exactly eighteen paths, all
# eighteen present in the archive, and this is that output.
#
# TWO FIELDS: A NUMBER AND A /usr-RELATIVE PATH.  The number is the name the
# copy is stored under, because sys/dir.h:2 is `#define DIRSIZ 14' and these
# are paths.  It is written down rather than counted, because six of these
# files are called y.tab.h and a basename would collide, and because a counter
# assigned inside `while read ... done < file' is lost in the subshell this
# shell forks for a redirected compound command.  A new row takes the next
# free number; ipnxbuild says so rather than overwriting if two rows share one.

# --- REGENERATED, AND THE TAPE SHIPS THE RESULT ANYWAY -------------------
# sys/io/mkfile's `%.x: %.c' is `cc $CFLAGS -S' then c2 and as, with the
# `rm $stem.s' COMMENTED OUT on the tape, so every .x member leaves its
# assembly in the source directory.  camac is the only one whose .s the tape
# also ships, and line 2 of that file is
# `.stabs "vaxpcc2",0xf0,0,17665,780003333' -- a date, 19 September 1994 --
# so a rebuild cannot reproduce the bytes even in principle.
